Never ask the Vulkan layer chain for entry points with a null instance (lsfg-vk Linux build, framegen/src/core/instance.cpp).

volkInitializeCustom() loads the global entry points (vkCreateInstance, ...) through the handler it is given, asking for them with
a null instance. The handler is the layer chain below lsfg-vk (Mesa's device-select layer on the phone), which reads its instance
table through that null instance and crashes with SIGSEGV at address 0x18. It showed as a crash of the game when frame generation
was started or changed while the game runs: an "Off" start (multiplier 1), switching Off -> 2x/3x/4x, and a swapchain recreation
after a change (the module is initialised again each time). An adopted instance needs only the instance level entry points, which
are asked for with the real instance, so the handler answers a null instance itself.

--- a/framegen/src/core/instance.cpp	2026-10-16 16:22:21
+++ b/framegen/src/core/instance.cpp	2026-10-16 16:22:21
@@ -48,8 +48,23 @@
     );
 }
 
+namespace {
+    PFN_vkGetInstanceProcAddr externalGipa = nullptr;
+
+    // volkInitializeCustom asks for the global entry points (vkCreateInstance, ...) with a null instance. The layer
+    // below a layer (Mesa's device-select) reads its instance table through that null instance and crashes
+    // (SIGSEGV, address 0x18). An adopted instance needs none of the global entry points: only the instance level
+    // ones, which are asked for with the real instance.
+    PFN_vkVoidFunction instanceOnlyGipa(VkInstance instance, const char* name) {
+        if (instance == VK_NULL_HANDLE)
+            return nullptr;
+        return externalGipa(instance, name);
+    }
+}
+
 Instance::Instance(PFN_vkGetInstanceProcAddr gipa, VkInstance external) {
-    volkInitializeCustom(gipa);
+    externalGipa = gipa;
+    volkInitializeCustom(instanceOnlyGipa);
     volkLoadInstance(external);
 
     // non-owning: the caller controls the instance lifetime
